// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO47 tagged "led_high" - confirmed onboard "TEST LED"
// (white, programmable). Vendor user manual (NAMINO-BIANCO-user-manual-
// M7102_02.pdf, section 4.1.1): "TEST LED is connected to ESP32 pin
// IO47." Polarity confirmed via vendor open-hardware KiCad schematic
// (Namino-BIANCO-HW_OdrprAn.pdf, WiFi.kicad_sch/sheet 5): LED2 wired
// PD2_IO47 -> R35 (2.4k) -> LED2 -> GND, the same resistor/LED part and
// topology as the always-on POWER LED (LED1: +3.3V -> R36 (2.4k) ->
// LED1 -> GND on PSU.kicad_sch/sheet 2) but fed from the GPIO instead of
// a permanent 3.3V rail - i.e. GPIO47 HIGH sources current through the
// LED to GND, so HIGH = LED on (active-high). pwm_output kept because
// GPIO47 already carries it in the chip-level default.
// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO0 tagged "button" - confirmed onboard "pushbutton
// for programming" (vendor user manual section 3.2 lists "1 pushbutton
// for programming"; section 5.1: "press the BT button to enter
// programming mode"). Vendor KiCad schematic (WiFi.kicad_sch/sheet 5)
// shows this "Program-button" (S2) driving a BOOT_PIN net through the
// standard CP2102N/BSS138 auto-reset level-shift circuit; BOOT_PIN
// corresponds to the WROOM module's IO0 pin, consistent with this
// board's own pinNotes ("0: Must be HIGH for normal boot" - the
// standard ESP32-S3 IO0 strapping requirement). Distinct from the
// separate physical RESET pushbutton, which drives the chip's EN/RESET
// pin (GRESET net, not a GPIO) and is therefore not taggable. Full copy
// of variants/esp32/esp32s3/board.json otherwise - no other pin
// exclusions needed for this board.
